WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.951
The Purdue Model matters because industrial operations depend on several kinds of systems working together.

00:00:06.951 --> 00:00:10.533
Enterprise planning may create a production schedule.

00:00:10.533 --> 00:00:14.114
Site operations translate that demand into work.

00:00:14.114 --> 00:00:21.065
Supervisory systems provide operator visibility, controllers execute logic, and field instruments interact with the process.

00:00:21.065 --> 00:00:28.859
A failure or compromise can move through these dependencies even when the systems belong to different teams.

00:00:28.859 --> 00:00:34.126
Placing functions into levels helps identify where connectivity should be limited.

00:00:34.126 --> 00:00:42.341
Traffic that stays within a control area has a different purpose from data being transferred to enterprise analytics.

00:00:42.341 --> 00:00:50.135
The model supports segmentation by showing where trust changes and where controlled intermediary services may be appropriate.

00:00:50.135 --> 00:00:52.874
It also supports risk analysis.

00:00:52.874 --> 00:00:58.140
Systems near the process may have direct safety and reliability consequences.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:06.355
Systems higher in the architecture may have broader user populations, internet dependencies, and exposure to common enterprise threats.

00:01:06.355 --> 00:01:13.307
Neither is automatically more important; their risk comes from different functions, pathways, and operational effects.

00:01:13.307 --> 00:01:16.466
The model improves communication between disciplines.

00:01:16.466 --> 00:01:21.311
Security teams can explain why direct enterprise-to-controller access creates risk.

00:01:21.311 --> 00:01:27.420
Engineers can explain why a control flow requires low latency or high availability.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:31.423
Operators can identify which systems provide essential visibility.

00:01:31.423 --> 00:01:36.689
These conversations turn an abstract network diagram into an operational architecture.

00:01:36.689 --> 00:01:41.113
Finally, Purdue helps distinguish data flow from control flow.

00:01:41.113 --> 00:01:49.328
Production data may move upward for reporting, while commands, recipes, or configuration changes move downward under stricter authority.

00:01:49.328 --> 00:01:57.122
Reviewing both directions reveals remote access, update, and administration paths that a simple data-flow discussion might miss.

00:01:57.122 --> 00:02:05.338
The goal is not to prevent communication, but to make every cross-level flow necessary, controlled, observable, and owned.
